# `$left.a == $right.b` keeps both columns; the same name on both sides is renamed with DS1007.
mode: check
show: schema
schema: 'orders.csv'(orderId: string, customerId: string, amount: real, qty: long, timestamp: datetime); 'customers.csv'(customerId: string, name: string, country: string, signupDate: datetime); 'c2.csv'(id: string, name: string)
---- query
from 'orders.csv' | join 'c2.csv' on $left.customerId == $right.id;
from 'orders.csv' | join 'c2.csv' on $right.id == $left.customerId;
from 'orders.csv' | join 'customers.csv' on $left.customerId == $right.customerId
---- expected
info[DS1007]: `join` renamed column `customerId` of the right table to `customerId1`
  --> query:3:26
  |
3 | from 'orders.csv' | join 'customers.csv' on $left.customerId == $right.customerId
  |                          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this table also has a column `customerId`
  |
  = help: to choose the names yourself, rename them on the right: `join (from 'customers.csv' | project-rename right_customerId = customerId)`
  = note: a column named in `on` is not renamed: the two sides make one column (only `$left.a == $right.b` keeps both)

query 1 columns:
  orderId: string
  customerId: string
  amount: real
  qty: long
  timestamp: datetime
  id: string
  name: string

query 2 columns:
  orderId: string
  customerId: string
  amount: real
  qty: long
  timestamp: datetime
  id: string
  name: string

query 3 columns:
  orderId: string
  customerId: string
  amount: real
  qty: long
  timestamp: datetime
  customerId1: string
  name: string
  country: string
  signupDate: datetime
